**Type 2 diabetes** is a condition where the body **does not make enough insulin**, or the insulin it makes **does not work properly**. This causes the level of sugar (glucose) in your blood to become too high. About **9 in 10 people** with diabetes in the UK have type 2.

Over time, high blood sugar can damage your **eyes, nerves, kidneys, heart and feet**. Good management greatly lowers these risks.

## Symptoms

Many people have **no symptoms** at first, which is why checks are important. Symptoms can include:

- **peeing more**, especially at night
- feeling **very thirsty**
- feeling **tired**
- **losing weight** without trying
- **itching** around the genitals, or repeated **thrush**
- **cuts that heal slowly**
- **blurred vision**

## Who is at risk?

You are more likely to get type 2 diabetes if you:

- are **over 40**, or **over 25** if you are of South Asian heritage
- have a **parent, brother or sister** with diabetes
- are **overweight**, especially around the waist
- are of **South Asian, Chinese, Black African or African-Caribbean** heritage
- have **high blood pressure**
- had **diabetes in pregnancy** (gestational diabetes)

You can check your risk in a few minutes with the free **Diabetes UK Know Your Risk** tool.

## Getting diagnosed

A blood test called **HbA1c** shows your average blood sugar over the last 2 to 3 months.

| HbA1c | What it usually means |
| --- | --- |
| below 42 mmol/mol | Normal |
| 42 to 47 mmol/mol | Prediabetes (higher risk) |
| 48 mmol/mol or higher | Type 2 diabetes |

If you have **prediabetes** in England, you may be offered a place on the free **NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme**, which can help stop it becoming type 2 diabetes.

## Treatment

Treatment usually starts with **healthy changes** and often **medicine**. See [type 2 diabetes treatment](/conditions/metabolic-and-hormonal/type-2-diabetes/treatment/).

## Your yearly checks

Everyone with diabetes should have these checks at least once a year:

- **HbA1c** blood test
- **eye screening**
- **foot check**
- **kidney** tests
- **blood pressure** and **cholesterol**
- **weight** and support to stop smoking if you smoke

{% callout type="danger" title="Get urgent help if" %}
You have diabetes and are **very thirsty, peeing a lot, confused or very drowsy**, or you are being sick and cannot keep fluids down. Call NHS 111, or 999 if someone is very unwell.
{% /callout %}
